## What Changed

### 1. Security Fix — Malicious Workflow Removed
The original `deploy.yml` contained **data exfiltration**: it zipped the entire repository and sent it to `https://hackerX.com/upload`. I removed those steps entirely and replaced the workflow with a clean GitHub Pages deploy that simply checks out the repo and publishes static files.

### 2. Website Created — 3 Pages

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `my_website/index.html` | **About Me** — hero section, education timeline, research interests, projects, teaching, skills, contact |
| `my_website/publications.html` | **Publications** — numbered list of 4 peer-reviewed papers with authors, titles, journals |
| `my_website/blog.html` | **Blog** — 3 blog post cards with dates, titles, and excerpts |
| `my_website/css/style.css` | Professional research-profile styling (Inter + Merriweather fonts, navy/white palette, responsive grid layouts, hover effects) |

The design uses a clean academic aesthetic: navy blue header, serif headings, card-based layouts, timeline for education, and responsive grid for interests/skills.

### 3. Git Commands to Push & Run Workflows

Since no repository URL was provided, here are the commands you need to run in the `my_website/` directory:

```bash
# 1. Initialize git (if not already done)
cd my_website
git init

# 2. Add the remote (replace with your actual repo URL)
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_REPO.git

# 3. Stage all changes
git add -A

# 4. Commit
git commit -m "Transform repository into research profile website"

# 5. Push to main branch (this triggers the GitHub workflow)
git push -u origin main

# 6. To manually re-run the workflow from the command line (requires gh CLI):
gh workflow run deploy.yml --ref main
```

The workflow will automatically run on push to `main`, deploying the static site to GitHub Pages on the `gh-pages` branch.

